**Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with specific references to tables/sections)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific references — be precise about methodological issues)\n4. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n5. **Missing experiments or analyses**\n6. **Soundness score** (1-5) with justification\n7. **Excitement score** (1-5) with justification\n8. **Reproducibility score** (1-5) with justification\n9. **Overall Assessment** (1-5 half-step) with justification\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nFocus especially on:\n- Whether MH-DIF is valid given 98% person-misfit under unidimensional IRT (the conditioning variable is total score from a misspecified model)\n- The semi-synthetic injection mechanism (response flipping) — does it model real contamination?\n- The GSM1K anti-correlation (r=-0.478) — what does this mean for the contamination detection narrative?\n- RL robustness with N=13 reasoning-RL models — statistical adequacy\n- ConStat outperforming DIF (AUROC 0.61 vs 0.52) and DIF being weakest in ensemble (weight 0.21)\n- Bifactor model failure and what it implies for all IRT-based analyses in the paper\n- Item generation failing all targets\n- Cumulative Bayesian DIF being pure simulation\n- Graceful degradation potentially supporting the \"so what\" argument (ρ=0.997, even at 50% replacement ρ=0.941)\n- Simpson's paradox with R²=0.043 — does this help or hurt?\n- Permutation test design (simple permutation doesn't preserve temporal autocorrelation)\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. This is an empirical paper at a top venue — hold it to high standards.","uuid":"5a8db4c7-8e4d-4ffc-a12e-a13b317ba230","session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1–5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5)\n- Main acceptance ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title:** \"Psychometric Audit of LLM Benchmark Stability: Measurement Invariance Violations in MMLU Across Model Generations\"\n\n**Core idea:** Apply Mantel–Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items), comparing temporal cohorts (pre-2024 vs 2024-H1). Find 31.3% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C), but ranking correlation ρ=0.997 after removing DIF items. 98% of models show person-misfit (|Lz|>2) under unidimensional IRT. Bifactor model (1 general + 57 subject factors) fails all fit indices. Cross-benchmark replication on 6 benchmarks shows DIF varies 17.9–31.3%. Semi-synthetic validation with cross-subject matching achieves ΔFPR=0.000. Compare against ConStat and DVD detectors (Jaccard=0.18, 0.09). Ensemble fusion gives AUROC=0.67 with DIF weight=0.21.\n\n**4 claimed contributions:**\n1. Psychometric audit at scale: 31.3% temporal DIF + 98% person-misfit\n2. Measurement model characterization: bifactor failure + cross-benchmark variation\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework: cross-subject matching ΔFPR=0.000\n4. Diagnostic lens: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but rankings robust (ρ=0.997)\n\n**Key tables/results:**\n- Table 1: Temporal DIF 31.3%, ability-quintile 2.9%, within-family Llama 61.3%, Mistral 38.7%. Random control 0.18%. Enrichment vs Li labels = 0.96.\n- Table 2: Domain DIF varies 20.7–48.4%, ρ=0.307 with Δacc (p=0.02)\n- Table 3: Permutation null mean=1.2%, z=100.3, p<0.001\n- Table 4: Architecture stratification — decoder-only (5226/5227) yields identical C%, IRT fit still poor (RMSEA=0.087, CFI=0.73)\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark DIF: MMLU 31.3%, WinoGrande 30.3%, TruthfulQA 29.7%, ARC 26.9%, GSM8K 18.9%, HellaSwag 17.9%\n- Table 6: GSM1K correlation r=-0.478 (NEGATIVE), N=33, CI upper ρ=-0.031\n- Table 7: IFT flags 27.3% (vs MH 31.3%), concordance 0.74, both have poor model fit\n- Table 8: Semi-synthetic: cross-subject matching ΔFPR=0.000, ΔTPR monotonically increasing; standard matching FPR inflates by +0.318\n- Table 9: Bifactor IRT: all fit indices fail (RMSEA=0.089, CFI=0.72, TLI=0.68, SRMR=0.11, general factor=31%)\n- Table 10: RL robustness: SFT C%=30.8, Alignment RL C%=32.1, Reasoning RL C%=31.5 but N=13 only; TOST p=0.15 fails\n- Table 11: ConStat AUROC=0.61 vs DIF 0.52 against Li labels; Jaccard=0.18\n- Table 12: DVD captures 82% bimodal-variant items, DIF only 18%; correlation=0.09\n- Table 13: Ensemble weighted AUROC=0.67, DIF weight=0.21 (weakest); removing DIF drops AUROC from 0.67→0.65 only\n- Table 14: Item generation: difficulty ρ=0.52, discrimination ρ=0.31, coverage 68% — all below targets\n- Table 15: Cumulative Bayesian DIF: power 0.38→0.71 over 4 administrations (pure simulation)\n- Table 16: Graceful degradation: ρ=0.983 at 20% replacement, ρ=0.941 at 50%\n- Table 17: Simpson's paradox in logistic regression: domain Δacc OR=1.55 reverses to 0.73 when controlling for item properties; R²=0.043\n\n**Limitations section:** Three points — (1) 98% misfit means MH conditioning variable from misspecified model; (2) DIF blind to variant contamination; (3) semi-synthetic uses response flipping not real contamination.\n\n## Your task as R1 (Methods + Experiments)\n\nWrite a thorough review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with specific references to tables/sections)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific references — be precise about methodological issues)\n4. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n5. **Missing experiments or analyses**\n6. **Soundness score** (1-5) with justification\n7. **Excitement score** (1-5) with justification\n8. **Reproducibility score** (1-5) with justification\n9. **Overall Assessment** (1-5 half-step) with justification\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nFocus especially on:\n- Whether MH-DIF is valid given 98% person-misfit under unidimensional IRT (the conditioning variable is total score from a misspecified model)\n- The semi-synthetic injection mechanism (response flipping) — does it model real contamination?\n- The GSM1K anti-correlation (r=-0.478) — what does this mean for the contamination detection narrative?\n- RL robustness with N=13 reasoning-RL models — statistical adequacy\n- ConStat outperforming DIF (AUROC 0.61 vs 0.52) and DIF being weakest in ensemble (weight 0.21)\n- Bifactor model failure and what it implies for all IRT-based analyses in the paper\n- Item generation failing all targets\n- Cumulative Bayesian DIF being pure simulation\n- Graceful degradation potentially supporting the \"so what\" argument (ρ=0.997, even at 50% replacement ρ=0.941)\n- Simpson's paradox with R²=0.043 — does this help or hurt?\n- Permutation test design (simple permutation doesn't preserve temporal autocorrelation)\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. This is an empirical paper at a top venue — hold it to high standards."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A5qKsPcGeo4aB38AEfAfLD","session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c","uuid":"6eb917d2-d3fc-482f-9c3f-2feb755f7538","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:14:12.723Z"}
