{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XZbGJdCG87Zz1wub8hySCM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\n2\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n3\t\n4\tMH-DIF analysis of six METABENCH benchmarks (27,125 items, 5,227 models) reveals 17.9--31.3\\% temporal item instability (ETS~C); a Simpson's paradox confirms item properties, not accuracy gains, drive DIF. Web-overlap labels are near-independent of DIF flags (enrichment $\\approx 0.96$); our semi-synthetic framework validates detection power ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$). DIF-flagged items concentrate discriminative information ($z{=}{-}8\\sigma$ to ${-}49\\sigma$) with a difficulty--direction interaction ($\\rho{=}{-}0.597$): gains on hard items, erosion on easy ones. LLM benchmarks need psychometric monitoring alongside score reporting.\n5\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A5qKsPcGeo4aB38AEfAfLD","session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c","uuid":"bb9491e3-d81d-43cf-8332-195b5694545c","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:14:37.504Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01915fLDTMwEnMU8geXGHdQp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PuS8GmEM35MEC2eKYJqRo8","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Produce a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1–5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5)\n- Main acceptance ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title:** \"Psychometric Audit of LLM Benchmark Stability: Measurement Invariance Violations in MMLU Across Model Generations\"\n\n**Core idea:** Apply Mantel–Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items), comparing temporal cohorts (pre-2024 vs 2024-H1). Find 31.3% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C), but ranking correlation ρ=0.997 after removing DIF items. 98% of models show person-misfit (|Lz|>2) under unidimensional IRT. Bifactor model (1 general + 57 subject factors) fails all fit indices (CFI=0.72, RMSEA=0.089). Cross-benchmark replication on 6 benchmarks shows DIF varies 17.9–31.3%. Semi-synthetic validation with cross-subject matching achieves ΔFPR=0.000.\n\n**4 claimed contributions:**\n1. Psychometric audit at scale: 31.3% temporal DIF + 98% person-misfit\n2. Measurement model characterization: bifactor failure + cross-benchmark variation (17.9–31.3%)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework: cross-subject matching ΔFPR=0.000\n4. Diagnostic lens: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but rankings robust (ρ=0.997)\n\n**Related Work covered (Section 2):**\n- LLM Benchmark Contamination: Dodge et al. 2021, Li et al. 2024, Deng et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2020 (n-gram), Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%), Dekoninck et al. 2024 (ConStat)\n- Psychometrics for LLMs: Polo et al. 2024, Vania et al. 2021, Zhuang et al. 2024\n- DIF in Educational Testing: Holland & Thayer 1993, Zumbo 1999, Mantel & Haenszel 1959, Zieky 1993, Tutz & Berger 2016\n- Benchmark Validity: Polo et al. 2024b (Growing Pains)\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- Table 1: Temporal DIF C%=31.3%, enrichment vs Li labels=0.96 (near unity — orthogonal to known contamination labels)\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark DIF varies nearly 2× (17.9–31.3%), coefficient of variation=0.22\n- Table 6: GSM1K correlation r=-0.478 (NEGATIVE — opposes contamination hypothesis)\n- Table 9: Bifactor IRT all indices fail\n- Table 11: ConStat AUROC=0.61 > DIF AUROC=0.52 against Li labels\n- Table 12: DVD correlation with DIF = 0.09 (near zero)\n- Table 13: Ensemble DIF weight=0.21 (weakest signal), incremental AUROC +0.02 only\n- Table 14: Item generation all metrics below targets\n- Table 15: Cumulative Bayesian DIF — pure simulation, no empirical validation\n- Table 16: Graceful degradation — ρ remains >0.94 even at 50% replacement\n- Table 17: Simpson's paradox, R²_McF=0.043 (95.7% variance unexplained)\n\n**Sections:** 1-Introduction, 2-Related Work, 3-Method (3.1 MH-DIF, 3.2 Semi-synthetic, 3.3 Person-fit), 4-Experiments (4.1-4.16, sixteen subsections), 5-Discussion, 6-Limitations, 7-Conclusion. 10 pages main text + 1 page tables. No appendix shown.\n\n## Your task as R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\nWrite a thorough review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with specific references)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific references — focus on novelty, positioning, and related work gaps)\n4. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n5. **Missing related work or positioning issues**\n6. **Soundness score** (1-5) with justification\n7. **Excitement score** (1-5) with justification\n8. **Reproducibility score** (1-5) with justification\n9. **Overall Assessment** (1-5 half-step) with justification\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nFocus especially on:\n- **Novelty assessment:** MH-DIF is a 1959/1988 method. Applying it to LLM benchmarks is novel application, but is the methodological contribution sufficient? The paper doesn't propose new methods — it applies existing psychometric tools.\n- **The \"so what\" problem:** ρ=0.997 means DIF doesn't affect rankings. The paper tries to reframe as \"redundancy finding\" but reviewers at top venues want actionable insights.\n- **Narrative coherence:** The paper oscillates between \"DIF detects instability\" and \"IRT is misspecified so DIF results may be artifacts.\" These two narratives undermine each other.\n- **Contribution deflation across experiments:** Many experiments produce negative or null results (bifactor fails, item generation fails targets, RL robustness underpowered, ensemble shows DIF is weakest, GSM1K anti-correlates). Does the paper have enough positive results to justify a main conference paper?\n- **Related work gaps:** Are there missing comparisons? What about Polo et al. 2024 (Growing Pains) and anchor-item stability? What about MIMIC models or propensity-score matching as alternatives to simple MH?\n- **Positioning vis-à-vis ConStat/DVD:** If ConStat outperforms DIF and DVD captures modes DIF misses, what is DIF's unique value proposition?\n- **The enrichment=0.96 result:** DIF flags are orthogonal to Li et al. web-overlap labels. Does this mean DIF detects something different or something spurious?\n- **Cross-benchmark variation (17.9–31.3%):** Does this suggest DIF is an artifact of benchmark multidimensionality rather than a universal phenomenon?\n- **Paper length and experiment density:** 16 experiment subsections in 10 pages — is the paper trying to cover too much ground, resulting in shallow treatment of each?\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated for a top NLP venue."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14370,"cache_read_input_tokens":42881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14370},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c","uuid":"df9c521e-5307-45ef-b5c4-7daec6cadcb6"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a77afb171720bb001","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PuS8GmEM35MEC2eKYJqRo8","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Produce a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1–5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5)\n- Main acceptance ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title:** \"Psychometric Audit of LLM Benchmark Stability: Measurement Invariance Violations in MMLU Across Model Generations\"\n\n**Core idea:** Apply Mantel–Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items), comparing temporal cohorts (pre-2024 vs 2024-H1). Find 31.3% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C), but ranking correlation ρ=0.997 after removing DIF items. 98% of models show person-misfit (|Lz|>2) under unidimensional IRT. Bifactor model (1 general + 57 subject factors) fails all fit indices (CFI=0.72, RMSEA=0.089). Cross-benchmark replication on 6 benchmarks shows DIF varies 17.9–31.3%. Semi-synthetic validation with cross-subject matching achieves ΔFPR=0.000.\n\n**4 claimed contributions:**\n1. Psychometric audit at scale: 31.3% temporal DIF + 98% person-misfit\n2. Measurement model characterization: bifactor failure + cross-benchmark variation (17.9–31.3%)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework: cross-subject matching ΔFPR=0.000\n4. Diagnostic lens: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but rankings robust (ρ=0.997)\n\n**Related Work covered (Section 2):**\n- LLM Benchmark Contamination: Dodge et al. 2021, Li et al. 2024, Deng et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2020 (n-gram), Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%), Dekoninck et al. 2024 (ConStat)\n- Psychometrics for LLMs: Polo et al. 2024, Vania et al. 2021, Zhuang et al. 2024\n- DIF in Educational Testing: Holland & Thayer 1993, Zumbo 1999, Mantel & Haenszel 1959, Zieky 1993, Tutz & Berger 2016\n- Benchmark Validity: Polo et al. 2024b (Growing Pains)\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- Table 1: Temporal DIF C%=31.3%, enrichment vs Li labels=0.96 (near unity — orthogonal to known contamination labels)\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark DIF varies nearly 2× (17.9–31.3%), coefficient of variation=0.22\n- Table 6: GSM1K correlation r=-0.478 (NEGATIVE — opposes contamination hypothesis)\n- Table 9: Bifactor IRT all indices fail\n- Table 11: ConStat AUROC=0.61 > DIF AUROC=0.52 against Li labels\n- Table 12: DVD correlation with DIF = 0.09 (near zero)\n- Table 13: Ensemble DIF weight=0.21 (weakest signal), incremental AUROC +0.02 only\n- Table 14: Item generation all metrics below targets\n- Table 15: Cumulative Bayesian DIF — pure simulation, no empirical validation\n- Table 16: Graceful degradation — ρ remains >0.94 even at 50% replacement\n- Table 17: Simpson's paradox, R²_McF=0.043 (95.7% variance unexplained)\n\n**Sections:** 1-Introduction, 2-Related Work, 3-Method (3.1 MH-DIF, 3.2 Semi-synthetic, 3.3 Person-fit), 4-Experiments (4.1-4.16, sixteen subsections), 5-Discussion, 6-Limitations, 7-Conclusion. 10 pages main text + 1 page tables. No appendix shown.\n\n## Your task as R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\nWrite a thorough review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with specific references)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific references — focus on novelty, positioning, and related work gaps)\n4. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n5. **Missing related work or positioning issues**\n6. **Soundness score** (1-5) with justification\n7. **Excitement score** (1-5) with justification\n8. **Reproducibility score** (1-5) with justification\n9. **Overall Assessment** (1-5 half-step) with justification\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nFocus especially on:\n- **Novelty assessment:** MH-DIF is a 1959/1988 method. Applying it to LLM benchmarks is novel application, but is the methodological contribution sufficient? The paper doesn't propose new methods — it applies existing psychometric tools.\n- **The \"so what\" problem:** ρ=0.997 means DIF doesn't affect rankings. The paper tries to reframe as \"redundancy finding\" but reviewers at top venues want actionable insights.\n- **Narrative coherence:** The paper oscillates between \"DIF detects instability\" and \"IRT is misspecified so DIF results may be artifacts.\" These two narratives undermine each other.\n- **Contribution deflation across experiments:** Many experiments produce negative or null results (bifactor fails, item generation fails targets, RL robustness underpowered, ensemble shows DIF is weakest, GSM1K anti-correlates). Does the paper have enough positive results to justify a main conference paper?\n- **Related work gaps:** Are there missing comparisons? What about Polo et al. 2024 (Growing Pains) and anchor-item stability? What about MIMIC models or propensity-score matching as alternatives to simple MH?\n- **Positioning vis-à-vis ConStat/DVD:** If ConStat outperforms DIF and DVD captures modes DIF misses, what is DIF's unique value proposition?\n- **The enrichment=0.96 result:** DIF flags are orthogonal to Li et al. web-overlap labels. Does this mean DIF detects something different or something spurious?\n- **Cross-benchmark variation (17.9–31.3%):** Does this suggest DIF is an artifact of benchmark multidimensionality rather than a universal phenomenon?\n- **Paper length and experiment density:** 16 experiment subsections in 10 pages — is the paper trying to cover too much ground, resulting in shallow treatment of each?\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated for a top NLP venue.","uuid":"275f7695-0e5c-437e-bc17-5d8070eb3012","session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Produce a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1–5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5)\n- Main acceptance ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title:** \"Psychometric Audit of LLM Benchmark Stability: Measurement Invariance Violations in MMLU Across Model Generations\"\n\n**Core idea:** Apply Mantel–Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items), comparing temporal cohorts (pre-2024 vs 2024-H1). Find 31.3% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C), but ranking correlation ρ=0.997 after removing DIF items. 98% of models show person-misfit (|Lz|>2) under unidimensional IRT. Bifactor model (1 general + 57 subject factors) fails all fit indices (CFI=0.72, RMSEA=0.089). Cross-benchmark replication on 6 benchmarks shows DIF varies 17.9–31.3%. Semi-synthetic validation with cross-subject matching achieves ΔFPR=0.000.\n\n**4 claimed contributions:**\n1. Psychometric audit at scale: 31.3% temporal DIF + 98% person-misfit\n2. Measurement model characterization: bifactor failure + cross-benchmark variation (17.9–31.3%)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework: cross-subject matching ΔFPR=0.000\n4. Diagnostic lens: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but rankings robust (ρ=0.997)\n\n**Related Work covered (Section 2):**\n- LLM Benchmark Contamination: Dodge et al. 2021, Li et al. 2024, Deng et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2020 (n-gram), Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%), Dekoninck et al. 2024 (ConStat)\n- Psychometrics for LLMs: Polo et al. 2024, Vania et al. 2021, Zhuang et al. 2024\n- DIF in Educational Testing: Holland & Thayer 1993, Zumbo 1999, Mantel & Haenszel 1959, Zieky 1993, Tutz & Berger 2016\n- Benchmark Validity: Polo et al. 2024b (Growing Pains)\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- Table 1: Temporal DIF C%=31.3%, enrichment vs Li labels=0.96 (near unity — orthogonal to known contamination labels)\n- Table 5: Cross-benchmark DIF varies nearly 2× (17.9–31.3%), coefficient of variation=0.22\n- Table 6: GSM1K correlation r=-0.478 (NEGATIVE — opposes contamination hypothesis)\n- Table 9: Bifactor IRT all indices fail\n- Table 11: ConStat AUROC=0.61 > DIF AUROC=0.52 against Li labels\n- Table 12: DVD correlation with DIF = 0.09 (near zero)\n- Table 13: Ensemble DIF weight=0.21 (weakest signal), incremental AUROC +0.02 only\n- Table 14: Item generation all metrics below targets\n- Table 15: Cumulative Bayesian DIF — pure simulation, no empirical validation\n- Table 16: Graceful degradation — ρ remains >0.94 even at 50% replacement\n- Table 17: Simpson's paradox, R²_McF=0.043 (95.7% variance unexplained)\n\n**Sections:** 1-Introduction, 2-Related Work, 3-Method (3.1 MH-DIF, 3.2 Semi-synthetic, 3.3 Person-fit), 4-Experiments (4.1-4.16, sixteen subsections), 5-Discussion, 6-Limitations, 7-Conclusion. 10 pages main text + 1 page tables. No appendix shown.\n\n## Your task as R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\nWrite a thorough review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with specific references)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific references — focus on novelty, positioning, and related work gaps)\n4. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n5. **Missing related work or positioning issues**\n6. **Soundness score** (1-5) with justification\n7. **Excitement score** (1-5) with justification\n8. **Reproducibility score** (1-5) with justification\n9. **Overall Assessment** (1-5 half-step) with justification\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nFocus especially on:\n- **Novelty assessment:** MH-DIF is a 1959/1988 method. Applying it to LLM benchmarks is novel application, but is the methodological contribution sufficient? The paper doesn't propose new methods — it applies existing psychometric tools.\n- **The \"so what\" problem:** ρ=0.997 means DIF doesn't affect rankings. The paper tries to reframe as \"redundancy finding\" but reviewers at top venues want actionable insights.\n- **Narrative coherence:** The paper oscillates between \"DIF detects instability\" and \"IRT is misspecified so DIF results may be artifacts.\" These two narratives undermine each other.\n- **Contribution deflation across experiments:** Many experiments produce negative or null results (bifactor fails, item generation fails targets, RL robustness underpowered, ensemble shows DIF is weakest, GSM1K anti-correlates). Does the paper have enough positive results to justify a main conference paper?\n- **Related work gaps:** Are there missing comparisons? What about Polo et al. 2024 (Growing Pains) and anchor-item stability? What about MIMIC models or propensity-score matching as alternatives to simple MH?\n- **Positioning vis-à-vis ConStat/DVD:** If ConStat outperforms DIF and DVD captures modes DIF misses, what is DIF's unique value proposition?\n- **The enrichment=0.96 result:** DIF flags are orthogonal to Li et al. web-overlap labels. Does this mean DIF detects something different or something spurious?\n- **Cross-benchmark variation (17.9–31.3%):** Does this suggest DIF is an artifact of benchmark multidimensionality rather than a universal phenomenon?\n- **Paper length and experiment density:** 16 experiment subsections in 10 pages — is the paper trying to cover too much ground, resulting in shallow treatment of each?\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated for a top NLP venue."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PuS8GmEM35MEC2eKYJqRo8","session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c","uuid":"bbc693c1-aa9d-49ff-a8c0-c5a8fb8d966f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:14:42.117Z"}
