{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0124U2wDwcEc9vBsB2ddEY8A","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n13\t\\usepackage{caption}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\\date{}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t% ============================================================\n24\t% ABSTRACT\n25\t% ============================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tLLM benchmarks implicitly assume that item difficulty is stable across model generations---when we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores measure the same construct.\n28\tWe test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\n29\tWe find that \\textbf{31.3\\%} of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category~C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts---a rate 142$\\times$ above the permutation null ($p<0.001$, $z=622$).\n30\tThis instability is robust across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%), not explained by capability improvement ($\\leq$2.4\\,pp of C\\%; $R^2=0.0007$), and not driven by model non-independence or architecture heterogeneity.\n31\tWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth is fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detection (enrichment $\\approx$1.0, $\\chi^2$ $p=0.258$), and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all tested subjects.\n32\tDespite 31\\% instability, MMLU rankings remain robust ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$), but DIF-item removal reveals systematic cohort bias ($z=8.5$)---DIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding \\emph{which} capability dimensions shift across generations, not a quality filter.\n33\t\\end{abstract}\n34\t\n35\t% ============================================================\n36\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n37\t% ============================================================\n38\t\\section{Introduction}\n39\t\n40\tLLM evaluation relies heavily on static benchmarks whose items are assumed to function identically regardless of when or which model is evaluated.\n41\tThis assumption---\\emph{temporal measurement invariance}---is rarely tested, yet its violation would undermine cross-generational score comparability.\n42\tPsychometrics has a mature tool for exactly this purpose: \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability \\citep{holland1993differential}.\n43\t\n44\tWe apply MH-DIF to METABENCH \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}, a repository of model-level response matrices, analyzing 5,227 models on 12,508 MMLU \\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} items.\n45\tThis is the largest DIF analysis conducted on LLM benchmarks.\n46\tOur key narrative principle: \\textbf{instability $\\neq$ invalidity}.\n47\tDIF flags items whose functioning changed across model generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.\n48\t\n49\tWe make four contributions:\n50\t\\begin{enumerate}\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{Temporal instability characterization}: 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large DIF between 2023 and 2024 cohorts, robust to threshold choice (10--49\\%), multiple testing (BH removes 3/3,918), and systematic confound elimination (architecture, ability gap, local dependence, model non-independence).\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Their enrichment $\\approx$1.0 against DIF flags is expected, not failure. Any item-level detector validated against these labels has this blind spot.\n53\t    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: A $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818).\n54\t    \\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z=-9.5$ to $-49\\sigma$) but are the least disruptive non-random removal strategy. Removing DIF items reveals systematic cohort bias ($z=8.5$), demonstrating actionable diagnostics for benchmark maintenance.\n55\t\\end{enumerate}\n56\t\n57\t% ============================================================\n58\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n59\t% ============================================================\n60\t\\section{Related Work}\n61\t\n62\t\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\n63\tToken-level methods (Min-K\\%: AUROC$=$0.72 on SFT, degrading to 0.51 on reasoning-RL models) and statistical approaches (ConStat: AUROC$=$0.62; DVD: AUROC$=$0.58--0.71) detect memorization but target different signals than DIF.\n64\tConStat's perturbation-sensitivity approach and DVD's generation-variance method are orthogonal to DIF's cohort-differential signal by construction (Jaccard overlap$=$0.15 between DIF and ConStat flags).\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Web-overlap ground truth.}\n67\t\\citet{li2024contamination} provide web-overlap labels for MMLU items, classifying whether items appear in public training corpora.\n68\tThese labels have become the \\emph{de facto} ground truth for contamination detection validation, but they measure global exposure rather than differential contamination---a distinction we formalize in \\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}.\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}\n71\tMH-DIF \\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988alternate} is the standard procedure for detecting items that function differently across demographic groups in high-stakes testing.\n72\tThe ETS classification system (A/B/C) combines statistical significance with effect-size thresholds \\citep{zieky1993practical}.\n73\tWe apply this framework without modification to LLM model cohorts, treating release date as the grouping variable.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Benchmark evaluation methodology.}\n76\tGrowing Pains \\citep{polo2024growing} uses anchor-item designs for efficient LLM evaluation.\n77\tOur DIF analysis is complementary: it identifies which anchor items are degrading over time.\n78\t\n79\t% ============================================================\n80\t% 3. METHOD\n81\t% ============================================================\n82\t\\section{Method}\n83\t\n84\t\\subsection{MH-DIF for LLM Benchmarks}\n85\t\n86\tGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times I}$ (models $\\times$ items), we partition models into reference (2023) and focal (2024-H1) cohorts.\n87\tFor each item $i$, the Mantel--Haenszel statistic tests whether the odds of correct response differ between cohorts after stratifying by total score (ability proxy).\n88\tThe MH odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\text{MH}}$ is converted to the $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$ effect-size metric on the ETS delta scale:\n89\t\\begin{equation}\n90\t    \\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})\n91\t\\end{equation}\n92\tItems are classified per ETS guidelines: Category~A ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|<1.0$ or not significant), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ and significant), C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$ and significant).\n93\tCategory~C items exhibit large DIF warranting review.\n94\t\n95\t\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n96\t\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n97\t\n98\tTo validate DIF detection power under controlled conditions, we inject known differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix.\n99\tFor a subset of focal-cohort models and target items, we flip incorrect responses to correct with probability $p$ (exploitation rate), simulating contamination at controlled intensity.\n100\tWe measure $\\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$ and $\\Delta$FPR $=$ FPR$(p)$ $-$ FPR$(0)$.\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{Cross-Subject Matching}\n103\t\\label{sec:crosssubject}\n104\t\n105\tStandard MH-DIF uses total score as the ability matching variable.\n106\tWhen contamination affects the same items used for matching, the matching variable itself becomes contaminated, inflating FPR.\n107\tWe propose \\emph{cross-subject matching}: for items in subject $s$, use total score on all subjects \\emph{except} $s$ as the matching variable.\n108\tThis eliminates matching variable contamination at the cost of slightly noisier ability estimation.\n109\t\n110\t\\textbf{Proposition 1.} Under cross-subject matching, injecting contamination into subject $s$ items does not alter the matching variable for those items.\n111\tTherefore $\\Delta$FPR $= 0$ for the contaminated subject, regardless of exploitation rate $p$.\n112\t\n113\t% ============================================================\n114\t% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n115\t% ============================================================\n116\t\\section{Experiments}\n117\t\n118\t\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n119\t\\label{sec:temporal}\n120\t\n121\tWe analyze the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohorts: reference ($N_{\\text{ref}}=1{,}080$, released before 2024) and focal ($N_{\\text{foc}}=807$, released 2024-H1).\n122\t\n123\t\\begin{table}[t]\n124\t\\centering\n125\t\\caption{Temporal DIF summary on MMLU. C\\% denotes the percentage of items classified as ETS Category~C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). Random C\\% is from within-cohort random splits.}\n126\t\\label{tab:temporal}\n127\t\\small\n128\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n129\t\\toprule\n130\tCohort & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & A\\% & B\\% & C\\% \\\\\n131\t\\midrule\n132\tTemporal 2023 vs.\\ 2024 & 1,080 & 807 & 45.3 & 23.4 & 31.3 \\\\\n133\tAbility Q1 vs.\\ Q5 & 2,608 & 2,608 & 82.1 & 15.0 & 2.9 \\\\\n134\tWithin-family Llama-2 vs.\\ 3 & 161 & 308 & 18.7 & 20.0 & 61.3 \\\\\n135\t\\midrule\n136\tRandom split (sanity) & -- & -- & -- & -- & 0.18 \\\\\n137\t\\bottomrule\n138\t\\end{tabular}\n139\t\\end{table}\n140\t\n141\tTable~\\ref{tab:temporal} presents the primary results.\n142\tThe temporal cohort shows 31.3\\% C-rate items (3,918 items; 95\\% bootstrap CI: [29.5, 33.1], $n=1{,}000$).\n143\tThe random-split sanity check yields C\\%$=$0.18\\%, confirming that the MH procedure itself does not inflate false positives.\n144\tThe ability-quintile cohort (Q1 vs.\\ Q5) shows only 2.9\\% C-rate (enrichment vs.\\ local independence$=$1.19), demonstrating that raw ability differences alone produce minimal DIF.\n145\tThe within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3, $d=-0.99$) yields 61.3\\%, providing an informative contrast for the ability-confound analysis in \\S\\ref{sec:confounds}.\n146\t\n147\tAll 57 MMLU subjects exhibit DIF (C\\% range: 20.7--48.4\\%, mean$=$30.6\\%, $\\text{SD}=5.5$; Kruskal--Wallis $p=0.78$), confirming benchmark-wide instability rather than subject-specific artifacts.\n148\t\n149\t\\begin{table}[t]\n150\t\\centering\n151\t\\caption{Domain-level DIF distribution (top/bottom subjects by C\\%).}\n152\t\\label{tab:domain}\n153\t\\small\n154\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}\n155\t\\toprule\n156\tSubject & $N$ & C\\% & \\%Fav.\\ 2024 & Domain \\\\\n157\t\\midrule\n158\tbusiness\\_ethics & 100 & 48.4 & 38.0 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\n159\thuman\\_aging & 223 & 41.9 & 34.5 & Other \\\\\n160\tformal\\_logic & 126 & 40.4 & 42.1 & Humanities \\\\\n161\thigh\\_school\\_stats & 216 & 42.0 & 55.3 & STEM \\\\\n162\t\\midrule\n163\tcomputer\\_security & 100 & 20.7 & 48.1 & STEM \\\\\n164\tmarketing & 234 & 20.9 & 40.2 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\n165\tmanagement & 103 & 21.7 & 43.7 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\n166\t\\bottomrule\n167\t\\end{tabular}\n168\t\\end{table}\n169\t\n170\tTable~\\ref{tab:domain} shows that high-DIF and low-DIF subjects span all domains.\n171\tA modest subject-level correlation exists between DIF rate and domain accuracy improvement ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$, $n=57$), but item-level decomposition (\\S\\ref{sec:confounds}) shows this is largely a Simpson's paradox artifact.\n172\t\n173\tSTEM subjects show higher rates of 2024-favoring DIF (55.4\\%) versus Humanities (35.6\\%), revealing domain-specific capability evolution patterns.\n174\t\n175\t\\subsection{Permutation Null and Statistical Robustness}\n176\t\\label{sec:permutation}\n177\t\n178\t\\begin{table}[t]\n179\t\\centering\n180\t\\caption{Permutation null distribution ($N=1{,}000$) vs.\\ observed temporal C\\%.}\n181\t\\label{tab:permutation}\n182\t\\small\n183\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n184\t\\toprule\n185\tStatistic & Value \\\\\n186\t\\midrule\n187\tObserved C\\% & 31.3 \\\\\n188\tPermutation null mean & 0.22 \\\\\n189\tPermutation null SD & 0.05 \\\\\n190\tPermutation null max & 0.41 \\\\\n191\t$z$-score & 622.0 \\\\\n192\t$p$-value & $<0.001$ \\\\\n193\tRandom-split control & 0.18 \\\\\n194\t\\bottomrule\n195\t\\end{tabular}\n196\t\\end{table}\n197\t\n198\tWe randomly reassign temporal labels 1,000 times (preserving $N_{\\text{ref}}=1{,}080$, $N_{\\text{foc}}=807$) and rerun MH-DIF (Table~\\ref{tab:permutation}).\n199\tThe observed 31.3\\% never appears in any permutation (null max$=$0.41\\%), yielding $z=622$ ($p<0.001$).\n200\tThe null mean (0.22\\%) closely matches the random-split control (0.18\\%), confirming both as valid null estimates."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A5qKsPcGeo4aB38AEfAfLD","session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c","uuid":"e3104a7f-b812-47aa-a44a-8e795a6d549b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:14:51.398Z"}
