{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KHm65sPtqTGDcrvnneWSHW","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/writing_plan.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"meta:\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\\\\n  track: long paper\\\\n  template: \\\\\\\"ACL 2023 / EMNLP 2026 double-column\\\\\\\"\\\\n  template_note: \\\\\\\"Switch from ICLR single-column to ACL double-column required\\\\\\\"\\\\n  page_limit: 8\\\\n  deadline: \\\\\\\"TBD (ARR cycle: June/August/October 2026)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  core_claim: >-\\\\n    Applying DIF to MMLU (5,227 models × 12,508 items) reveals 31% item-level\\\\n    functional instability across model generations, exposes a fundamental gap\\\\n    in contamination ground truth labels (empirically validated: enrichment=0.964,\\\\n    Jaccard=0.206), and provides a semi-synthetic validation framework for\\\\n    item-level detectors.\\\\n  created_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T23:45:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  story_arc:\\\\n    hook: >-\\\\n      When we say \\\\\\\"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\\\\\\\" we assume temporal\\\\n      comparability — but 31% of MMLU items function differently across model\\\\n      generations.\\\\n    gap: >-\\\\n      Existing contamination labels (web-overlap, n-gram) measure global\\\\n      exposure, not differential item functioning — every validation study\\\\n      using these labels has a blind spot. This is a fundamental validation\\\\n      methodology gap affecting the entire contamination detection field.\\\\n    insight: >-\\\\n      DIF detects differential contamination (semi-synthetic ΔTPR=+0.508) but\\\\n      temporal DIF is dominated by genuine capability evolution\\\\n      (enrichment≈1.0, within-subject C%=38.1%), and three structural\\\\n      corrections cannot separate them.\\\\n    mechanism: >-\\\\n      Semi-synthetic injection into real response matrices provides controlled\\\\n      ground truth; cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000).\\\\n    evidence: >-\\\\n      31% items functionally unstable (plan_001/014); ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5\\\\n      (plan_011); ΔFPR=0.000 cross-subject (plan_016); three improvements\\\\n      exhausted (plan_001b/012/014).\\\\n\\\\npaper_outline:\\\\n  - section: abstract\\\\n    word_budget: 200\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Problem: LLM benchmark scores assume temporal comparability, but\\\\n        item-level behavior may drift across model generations\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Approach: Apply MH-DIF with ETS classification to METABENCH\\\\n        (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Finding 1: ~31% items functionally unstable across generations\\\\n        (31.3% temporal cohort primary, within-subject 31-45% avg 38.1%)\\\\n        — genuine capability evolution, not statistical artifact (sanity\\\\n        check 0.18%). [Architecture-stratified TBD: plan_010 running]\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Finding 2: Web-overlap contamination labels do not capture\\\\n        differential contamination (enrichment≈1.0); semi-synthetic\\\\n        injection confirms DIF detection power (ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5).\\\\n        Validation methodology gap affects entire field.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Finding 3: Semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject\\\\n        matching (ΔFPR=0.000) as reusable infrastructure; GSM8K replication\\\\n        confirms cross-benchmark portability (ΔTPR=+0.818)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Finding 4: DIF-flagged items concentrate cross-generational\\\\n        discriminative information — removing them disrupts rankings MORE\\\\n        than random removal (z=-8 to -49σ), while overall rankings stay\\\\n        robust (ρ>0.996). Cross-benchmark validation: DIF flags\\\\n        anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap (r=−0.478, 95%\\\\n        CI=[−0.71, −0.16], p=0.005) — DIF-C items resist contamination-\\\\n        based performance gains (suggestive, N=33).\\\\n        DIF is diagnostic lens, not item filter.\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, method, discussion]\\\\n    registry_refs:\\\\n      - plan_001_real_data_dif_mmlu\\\\n      - plan_011_semi_synthetic_validation\\\\n      - plan_014_within_subject_dif\\\\n      - plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic\\\\n      - plan_017_downstream_impact\\\\n      - plan_004_gsm8k_cross_benchmark\\\\n\\\\n  - section: introduction\\\\n    word_budget: 1200\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Hook: MMLU scores are treated as directly comparable across time —\\\\n        but 31% of items behave differently for 2024 vs 2023 model\\\\n        cohorts (ETS C DIF), persisting within every subject domain\\\\n        (31-45%, avg 38.1%)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Gap: Contamination detection research validates against web-overlap\\\\n        labels, but these measure global exposure, not differential item\\\\n        functioning — a fundamental validation methodology gap. All work\\\\n        using web-overlap/n-gram labels as ground truth for item-level\\\\n        detectors (including Min-K%, n-gram methods) has the same blind\\\\n        spot.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Insight: Applying classical psychometric DIF to LLM benchmarks\\\\n        reveals that item-level behavioral differences are pervasive and\\\\n        structural — three correction attempts (matched-ability,\\\\n        θ-conditioning, bifactor pre-check) all fail, pointing to genuine\\\\n        capability evolution as the dominant source\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Tension resolution (Dream #3, MUST address in §1 end): \\\\\\\"31%\\\\n        items unstable\\\\\\\" sounds alarming but \\\\\\\"ρ=0.997 ranking correlation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        sounds reassuring. Resolve upfront: instability is real but\\\\n        concentrated in redundant measurement → benchmark robust at\\\\n        ranking level, fragile at item level. This is precisely why DIF\\\\n        is a diagnostic lens (understand what changed) not a quality\\\\n        filter (remove bad items).\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Discovery process (anti-post-hoc framing): \\\\\\\"We initially\\\\n        hypothesized DIF could detect contaminated items. Three structural\\\\n        improvements failed to separate contamination from capability\\\\n        evolution. This led us to investigate what persistent DIF actually\\\\n        captures.\\\\\\\" Pivot was data-driven: plan_011 semi-synthetic success\\\\n        (ΔTPR=+0.508) proved DIF works but ground truth labels don't —\\\\n        triggering problem redefinition. New evidence (plan_017 z=-49σ,\\\\n        plan_004 r=−0.478) came from post-pivot experiments, not\\\\n        reinterpretation of old data.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Novelty statement (MUST be explicit per Dream #3): We do not\\\\n        modify MH-DIF itself. Our novelty: (a) unprecedented scale\\\\n        (5,227 models) creates statistical challenges absent in\\\\n        educational testing; (b) cross-subject matching innovation\\\\n        eliminates FPR inflation; (c) ground truth meta-critique\\\\n        exposing validation methodology gap; (d) largest empirical\\\\n        characterization of item-level benchmark behavior.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Contributions: (C1) Benchmark temporal comparability concern —\\\\n        31% functional instability as empirical finding; (C2) Ground\\\\n        truth meta-critique — validation methodology gap in contamination\\\\n        detection literature; (C3) Semi-synthetic validation framework\\\\n        with cross-subject matching; (C4) DIF items concentrate\\\\n        discriminative information — diagnostic lens, not item filter\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments]\\\\n    figures: [fig_1_global_vs_differential]\\\\n    citation_hints:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"METABENCH dataset citation\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Li et al. (EMNLP 2024) web-overlap labels\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Wang et al. (ICLR 2026) RL fragility — motivates psychometric approach\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Growing Pains (Habba et al., NOT Polo) — positioning: they do score comparability, we do temporal stability characterization\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: related_work\\\\n    word_budget: 800\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Cluster 1: Contamination detection methods (Min-K%, ConStat, DVD,\\\\n        Self-Critique, n-gram baselines) — all validate against global\\\\n        exposure labels, not differential contamination; our meta-critique\\\\n        applies to their validation methodology\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Cluster 2: IRT in LLM evaluation (Growing Pains [Habba et al.],\\\\n        PSN-IRT [Zhou et al.], Hardy scalability, Lalor 2019, Vania\\\\n        2021) — DEDICATED technical comparison with Growing Pains: they\\\\n        use anchor items for efficient extensible evaluation; our DIF\\\\n        diagnostic tells them WHICH anchor items are degrading across\\\\n        model generations. Mutual value: their framework + our\\\\n        diagnostic = monitored extensible evaluation. Complementary not\\\\n        competing. (R2-W2)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Cluster 3: DIF methodology — MH-DIF (Holland & Thayer 1988), ETS\\\\n        classification, non-uniform DIF (Swaminathan & Rogers 1990),\\\\n        IRT-based alternatives (Lord's χ², Raju's area), SIBTEST (Shealy\\\\n        & Stout 1993), logistic regression DIF (Clauser & Mazor 1998,\\\\n        Zumbo 1999), small-sample DIF (Belzak 2020), propensity score\\\\n        matching for DIF (Teresi 2007). Our application context is novel;\\\\n        method itself is established; we acknowledge impact-vs-DIF\\\\n        confound as central challenge.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Cluster 4: Measurement invariance (Meredith 1993, Vandenberg &\\\\n        Lance 2000) — DIF is item-level test of measurement invariance;\\\\n        our work is the first large-scale measurement invariance analysis\\\\n        of LLM benchmarks. Frame DIF within broader invariance testing\\\\n        tradition.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Cluster 5: Benchmark design and concurrent work (LiveBench,\\\\n        GAIA, Chatbot Arena, dynamic benchmarks) — we characterize\\\\n        existing benchmarks psychometrically, they build new ones or\\\\n        use alternative evaluation paradigms\\\\n    dependencies: [introduction]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: method\\\\n    word_budget: 1400\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        3.1 Data: METABENCH response matrix (5,227 × 12,508) + Li et al.\\\\n        web-overlap labels (73.8% coverage, 9,229 items)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        3.2 MH-DIF + ETS A/B/C classification — translate for ML audience:\\\\n        examinees→models, test items→benchmark questions, DIF→item-level\\\\n        behavioral anomaly conditional on ability. ETS thresholds: A\\\\n        (|Δ|<1.0), B (1.0≤|Δ|<1.5), C (|Δ|≥1.5)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        3.3 Cohort designs: temporal (2023 vs 2024, N=1253/656),\\\\n        ability-quintile (Q1-2 vs Q4-5), within-family (Llama-2 N=161 vs\\\\n        Llama-3 N=308), within-subject (per-MMLU-subject DIF)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        3.4 Semi-synthetic injection framework: select baseline-clean\\\\n        items (ETS A in uninjected data), inject contamination by flipping\\\\n        focal-group incorrect→correct responses at rate p ∈ {0.3,0.5,0.7,1.0},\\\\n        measure ΔTPR = TPR(injected) - TPR(baseline). Cross-subject\\\\n        matching: use total score on OTHER subjects as matching variable,\\\\n        avoiding matching variable contamination.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        3.5 Diagnostics: Yen's Q3 for local independence, domain-specificity\\\\n        analysis (Spearman ρ between C% and accuracy improvement by subject)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        3.6 Multiple testing considerations (Dream #3 required): ETS\\\\n        classification provides implicit protection via dual threshold\\\\n        (effect size |Δ_MH|≥1.5 AND significance p<0.05). BH FDR q=0.05\\\\n        correction removes only 3/3918 items (31.32%→31.30%) — ETS dual\\\\n        threshold already controls FPR. Random-split sanity check\\\\n        (C%=0.18%). Threshold sensitivity: C%=48.9% at 1.0, 31.3% at\\\\n        1.5 (standard), 18.3% at 2.0, 10.2% at 2.5. \\\\\\\"Finding robust\\\\n        at standard thresholds; even at conservative 2.5, 1,278 items\\\\n        still exhibit large DIF.\\\\\\\" (plan_018). Architecture\\\\n        heterogeneity: non-issue (plan_010, 99.98% decoder-only).\\\\n    dependencies: []\\\\n    notation_table:\\\\n      θ: \\\\\\\"latent ability (IRT)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      Δ_MH: \\\\\\\"Mantel-Haenszel DIF statistic (log-odds ratio)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      p: \\\\\\\"exploitation rate in semi-synthetic injection\\\\\\\"\\\\n      \\\\\\\"C%\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"percentage of items classified ETS C (large DIF)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ΔTPR: \\\\\\\"TPR increase from injection over baseline\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ΔFPR: \\\\\\\"FPR change from injection (should be 0 for valid framework)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: experiments\\\\n    word_budget: 2500\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        4.1 Validation: DIF works under controlled conditions — power\\\\n        analysis with real MMLU IRT parameters, ETS C TPR=0.68-0.80 at\\\\n        N≥80 δ≥1.4, FPR=0.036-0.042, AUC 0.93-0.94\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        4.2 Temporal comparability concern: 31.3% ETS C (temporal,\\\\n        primary estimate; BH-corrected 31.30%), sanity random split\\\\n        0.18%, within-family Llama-2v3 61.3% (ability confound),\\\\n        ability-quintile 2.9% (well-controlled), domain ρ=0.307\\\\n        (p=0.02). Within-subject C%=38.1% (31-45% across 5 subjects)\\\\n        — instability penetrates within every domain. Instability\\\\n        decomposition (plan_019): ρ=0.307 is ecological correlation;\\\\n        item-level domain improvement explains only 2.4pp of 31.3%\\\\n        (pseudo R²=0.0007, counterfactual C%=29.0%). 92.4% of\\\\n        instability is unrelated to domain accuracy improvement.\\\\n        Use Model 1 (no causal assumptions) as primary; Model 2\\\\n        shows Simpson's paradox (supplementary).\\\\n        Architecture heterogeneity (plan_010): non-issue — METABENCH\\\\n        MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227), excluding the 1\\\\n        non-decoder model yields C%=31.32% (Jaccard=1.0 with full\\\\n        population). IRT person-fit (98% |Lz|>2) reflects MMLU's\\\\n        57-subject multidimensionality, not architecture heterogeneity.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        4.3 Ground truth mismatch: enrichment=0.96 against Li et al.\\\\n        web-overlap labels; semi-synthetic ΔTPR=+0.508 (p=0.5), +0.674\\\\n        (p=1.0), clear dose-response; DIF WORKS for differential\\\\n        contamination but Li et al. labels are the wrong ground truth\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        4.4 Structural analysis — empirical observation that three\\\\n        structural improvements fail: (a) matched-ability enrichment\\\\n        0.94-1.04 (main text 1 sentence → Appendix A.x for detail),\\\\n        (b) θ-conditioning r=0.9986 FPR unchanged at 31% (Appendix A.y\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Structural Constraints\\\\\\\"), (c) within-subject pre-check C%=38.1%\\\\n        (bifactor not viable). Frame as: jointly constraining DIF utility,\\\\n        not formal impossibility. [Reviewer allocation: plan_001b main=1\\\\n        sentence, plan_012 appendix-only]\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        4.5 Semi-synthetic framework validation: cross-subject matching\\\\n        ΔFPR=0.000 all 5 subjects, 4/5 ΔTPR>0.3 at p=0.5 (cross), vs\\\\n        within-subject FPR inflation +0.186. Cross-subject matching as\\\\n        methodological recommendation.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        4.6 Q3 local independence: 11.3% within-subject pairs >0.20 vs\\\\n        2.2% cross-subject (5x, p=2.3e-9); mild local dependence,\\\\n        contributing factor but not main FPR driver\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        4.7 Downstream impact — four layers: (1) Benchmark redundancy:\\\\n        ρ≈0.997 full vs stable-only, rankings extremely robust to 31%\\\\n        removal; (2) DIF items carry more signal than average items\\\\n        (z=-8 to -49σ vs random, 21/21), BUT circularity ablation\\\\n        (plan_020) shows DIF is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy:\\\\n        high-discrimination z=-15.8, accuracy-gap z=-156.5, high-variance\\\\n        z=-107.9 vs DIF z=-9.5. DIF specifically isolates ability-\\\\n        conditioned differential behavior, not just generally informative\\\\n        items. DIF-discrimination Jaccard=0.12 (orthogonal constructs).\\\\n        DO NOT simultaneously claim \\\\\\\"signal carriers\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"conservative\\\\n        filter\\\\\\\" — use: \\\\\\\"DIF items carry more signal than average but\\\\n        DIF isolates differential behavior specifically.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        (3) DIF as diagnostic lens: identifies which capability\\\\n        dimensions drive ranking shifts across generations. Model-specific\\\\n        patterns (phi-3/llama-3 +700-850, mistral merges -400 to -587)\\\\n        but caveat: top-50 movers 49/50 \\\\\\\"unknown\\\\\\\" family;\\\\n        (4) Cross-benchmark validation: GSM1K contamination gap\\\\n        anti-correlates with DIF advantage (r=−0.478, 95% CI=[−0.71,\\\\n        −0.16], p=0.005) — DIF-C items resist contamination-based\\\\n        performance gains (N=33, partial r=−0.385 controlling accuracy,\\\\n        permutation p=0.014). Suggestive not conclusive; item difficulty\\\\n        confound cannot be fully excluded at N=33.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        4.8 Cross-benchmark validation (GSM8K, 1,319 items × 6,702 models):\\\\n        Three-part analysis. (a) Temporal DIF: C%=18.9% (standard matching),\\\\n        sanity 0.3% — instability is lower than MMLU (31.3%) but still\\\\n        substantial for a single-domain benchmark. (b) Semi-synthetic:\\\\n        ΔTPR=+0.818 (standard, p=0.7), +0.517 (external, p=0.7); dose-\\\\n        response confirmed; framework generalizes beyond MMLU.\\\\n        (c) GSM1K contamination gap correlation: DIF-C items anti-correlate\\\\n        with contamination gap (r=−0.478, 95% CI=[−0.71, −0.16], p=0.005;\\\\n        partial r=−0.385 controlling accuracy; permutation p=0.014, N=33).\\\\n        MUST honestly report: original hypothesis predicted POSITIVE\\\\n        correlation (contaminated models gain on DIF items); observed\\\\n        NEGATIVE direction — DIF-C items resist contamination-based gains.\\\\n        Suggestive at N=33; item difficulty confound not fully excluded.\\\\n        [Reviewer: 1 paragraph + fig_5, honest hypothesis vs result]\\\\n    dependencies: []\\\\n    registry_refs:\\\\n      - mvp_dif_power_sim\\\\n      - mvp_dif_power_sim_phase2\\\\n      - plan_001_real_data_dif_mmlu\\\\n      - plan_001b_matched_ability_dif\\\\n      - plan_011_semi_synthetic_validation\\\\n      - plan_012_irt_theta_conditioning\\\\n      - plan_013_q3_local_independence\\\\n      - plan_014_within_subject_dif\\\\n      - plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic\\\\n      - plan_017_downstream_impact\\\\n      - plan_018_ets_threshold_sensitivity\\\\n      - plan_019_instability_decomposition\\\\n      - plan_010_architecture_stratified_dif\\\\n      - plan_020_circularity_ablation\\\\n      - plan_004_gsm8k_cross_benchmark\\\\n    tables: [tab_1_temporal_landscape, tab_2_structural_comparison]\\\\n    figures: [fig_2_dose_response, fig_3_within_subject_matching, fig_4_downstream_impact, fig_5_gsm1k_dif_correlation]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: discussion\\\\n    word_budget: 1000\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        5.1 Implications for benchmark temporal comparability: 31%\\\\n        instability (robust across thresholds: 10-49% at ETS 2.5-1.0)\\\\n        means cross-temporal MMLU scores carry uncontrolled item-level\\\\n        variance; instability decomposition shows 92.4% unrelated to\\\\n        domain accuracy improvement; benchmark designers should monitor\\\\n        DIF as quality diagnostic\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        5.2 Ground truth meta-critique — AGGRESSIVE framing per Director:\\\\n        all work validating item-level detectors against web-overlap/n-gram\\\\n        labels has the same validation methodology gap. Web-overlap measures\\\\n        existence, not differential usage. This is a meta-contribution to\\\\n        the contamination detection field — not just \\\\\\\"DIF and Li et al.\\\\n        measure different things\\\\\\\" but \\\\\\\"the field's standard validation\\\\n        approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        5.3a DIF as capability diagnostic (Claim 4 layer 4 discussion):\\\\n        GSM1K negative correlation reveals contamination produces \\\\\\\"shallow\\\\\\\"\\\\n        performance boost concentrated on non-DIF items, while DIF-C items\\\\n        capture genuine capability differences between model generations.\\\\n        171/249 focal-favoring DIF-C items (α<1) drive the effect — these\\\\n        are items where 2024 models genuinely improved, and contaminated\\\\n        models struggle disproportionately on them. MUST caveat: r=−0.478\\\\n        with CI=[−0.71, −0.16] is suggestive; item difficulty confound\\\\n        (DIF-C items may simply be harder) not fully excluded; N=33 limits\\\\n        generalizability. This is a paper highlight per Director but\\\\n        requires honest strength calibration.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        5.3b Empirical observation on DIF applicability: 1D IRT fit\\\\n        limitations + power requirements + ability confounding jointly\\\\n        constrain DIF utility in LLM benchmarks. This is a structural\\\\n        constraint revealed by systematic experimentation, NOT a formal\\\\n        impossibility — higher-level methods (MIMIC, propensity-score\\\\n        matching, multidimensional IRT) may address individual constraints.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        5.4 Limitations — MUST BE HONEST, non-negotiable:\\\\n        (a) ρ=0.307 ecological correlation between C% and accuracy\\\\n        improvement, but item-level decomposition (plan_019) shows\\\\n        domain improvement explains only 2.4pp (7.6%) of 31.3% C%.\\\\n        Still, cannot fully decompose remaining 92.4% into\\\\n        contamination vs other sources of capability change;\\\\n        (b) 31% C% cannot be decomposed into contamination vs capability;\\\\n        (c) semi-synthetic injection (incorrect→correct flipping) may\\\\n        not match real contamination patterns (partial knowledge, format\\\\n        memorization);\\\\n        (d) primarily MMLU/METABENCH — GSM8K cross-validation (§4.8)\\\\n        confirms semi-synthetic framework portability and provides GSM1K\\\\n        correlation evidence (N=33), but full temporal DIF characterization\\\\n        on diverse benchmarks not yet done; GSM1K N=33 has wide CI;\\\\n        (e) cross-subject baseline FPR 33-53% — stable across injection\\\\n        levels but not low;\\\\n        (f) RL robustness: theoretically motivated but empirically\\\\n        unvalidated with adequate sample size (N=13 reasoning-RL models\\\\n        in METABENCH);\\\\n        (g) downstream impact family analysis limited to N≥20 families\\\\n        (llama, mistral, qwen, phi); top-50 rank movers 49/50 are\\\\n        \\\\\\\"unknown\\\\\\\" family — family-level conclusions have limited coverage\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        5.5 Future work (SCOPE REDUCED per Dream #3 — main text only\\\\n        lists directions, no extended discussion): broader cross-\\\\n        benchmark generalization, RL model cohorts with adequate N,\\\\n        MIMIC models for confound control. [Appendix-only: Bayesian DIF,\\\\n        ensemble fusion (DIF + ConStat + DVD), complementary baselines,\\\\n        pilot studies (bifactor equating, item generation), SIBTEST/LR-DIF\\\\n        comparison, item-focused trees]\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: conclusion\\\\n    word_budget: 350\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Four contributions: (1) 38% MMLU items functionally unstable\\\\n        across model generations, (2) ground truth meta-critique —\\\\n        validation methodology gap in contamination detection, (3)\\\\n        semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching (cross-\\\\n        benchmark replication on GSM8K: ΔTPR=+0.818), (4) DIF as\\\\n        diagnostic lens — four-layer finding\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Key message: LLM benchmarks need psychometric monitoring, not\\\\n        just score reporting — DIF reveals benchmark quality as a\\\\n        benchmark quality tool\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        DIF items concentrate discriminative information (removal disrupts\\\\n        rankings MORE than random, z=-8 to -49σ) and anti-correlate with\\\\n        contamination gap (r=−0.478, CI=[−0.71, −0.16]) — DIF-C items\\\\n        resist contamination-based performance gains (suggestive, N=33)\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, discussion]\\\\n\\\\nvisualization_plan:\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_1_global_vs_differential\\\\n    type: conceptual\\\\n    tool: paperbanana\\\\n    description: >-\\\\n      Global vs Differential contamination concept diagram with ICC\\\\n      illustration — global exposure affects all models equally (no DIF),\\\\n      differential training data inclusion creates group-specific ICC\\\\n      shifts (DIF detected). Shows why enrichment≈1.0 is expected.\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      Web-overlap labels and DIF measure fundamentally different things:\\\\n      global exposure vs differential item functioning. Low enrichment is\\\\n      expected, not failure.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Left panel: \\\\\\\"Global Contamination\\\\\\\" — item on Common Crawl since\\\\n        2020, both cohort ICCs overlap (no DIF), Li et al. labels it\\\\n        contaminated but DIF correctly reports no group difference\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Right panel: \\\\\\\"Differential Contamination\\\\\\\" — item in focal\\\\n        cohort's training data only, ICCs diverge (DIF detected), Li\\\\n        et al. may or may not label it\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Use ICC curves (S-shaped logistic) with shaded area between\\\\n        curves = DIF magnitude (Δ_MH)\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Color: reference cohort blue, focal cohort orange (Okabe-Ito)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Bottom annotation connecting panels: \\\\\\\"Enrichment ≈ 1.0 because\\\\n        most MMLU items are globally contaminated (left), not\\\\n        differentially (right)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: introduction\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"16:9\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_2_dose_response\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >-\\\\n      Semi-synthetic dose-response: TPR vs exploitation rate p for\\\\n      temporal cohort with external/cross-subject matching, plus FPR\\\\n      stability panel\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      DIF detection power increases monotonically with contamination\\\\n      exploitation rate (clear dose-response), and cross-subject matching\\\\n      maintains perfectly stable FPR (ΔFPR=0.000) — controlled detection\\\\n      capability confirmed.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"x-axis: exploitation rate p ∈ {0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0}\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Top panel: TPR lines — temporal_external (primary, solid blue),\\\\n        temporal_standard (dashed red, shows FPR inflation problem at\\\\n        high p)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Bottom panel (smaller): FPR vs p — external flat at ~0.31\\\\n        (ΔFPR=0.000), standard inflating from 0.30 to 0.62\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern A: light regime band at p<0.3 labeled 'below practical detection'\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dashed horizontal lines at baseline TPR (p=0) for reference\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Annotate ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 on the primary line\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_011_semi_synthetic_validation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"3:4\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_3_within_subject_matching\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >-\\\\n      Within-subject semi-synthetic: 5 subjects comparing cross-subject\\\\n      vs within-subject matching on ΔTPR and ΔFPR\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 for\\\\n      all 5 subjects) while maintaining detection power — a practical\\\\n      methodological recommendation.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Paired dot plot or grouped bars: 5 subjects on x-axis, sorted\\\\n        by cross-subject ΔTPR\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Left panel: ΔTPR at p=0.5 — cross (filled circle) vs within\\\\n        (open circle), connected by line\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Right panel: ΔFPR — cross all at 0.000 (flat line), within\\\\n        showing inflation (+0.067 to +0.281)\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Reference line: ΔTPR=0.3 (success threshold), ΔFPR=0\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Highlight business_ethics: high baseline FPR (0.53-0.56),\\\\n        negative within-subject ΔTPR, low cross-subject ΔTPR — the\\\\n        failure case\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"3:2\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_4_downstream_impact\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >-\\\\n      Model-level rank change scatter: full-benchmark rank vs\\\\n      stable-only rank, showing that DIF removal systematically\\\\n      disrupts rankings more than random removal. Annotate key model\\\\n      clusters (phi-3/llama-3 beneficiaries, mistral merge losers).\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      DIF-flagged items concentrate cross-generational discriminative\\\\n      information — removing them disrupts rankings MORE than random\\\\n      removal, revealing model-specific evolution patterns rather than\\\\n      benchmark noise.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Scatter plot: x = full-benchmark rank, y = stable-only rank.\\\\n        Diagonal line (y=x) = no change. Points above = gained rank\\\\n        (benefited from DIF items), below = lost rank.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Color by model family or cohort (2023 blue, 2024 orange) if\\\\n        feasible; otherwise uniform gray with key models highlighted\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Annotate clusters: phi-3 / llama-3 derivatives (large positive\\\\n        rank change, +700-850), mistral merges (large negative, -400\\\\n        to -587)\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Inset or annotation box: ρ_full_vs_stable = 0.996, z_dif_vs_random\\\\n        = -14.9 (mean), DIF items 1.09× cross-cohort ratio\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Reference diagonal dashed line y=x\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Alternative: if scatter too dense, use hexbin or contour\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_017_downstream_impact\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_5_gsm1k_dif_correlation\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >-\\\\n      GSM1K contamination gap vs DIF advantage scatter: 33 matched models,\\\\n      showing significant negative correlation (r=−0.478, p=0.005).\\\\n      Models with higher contamination (GSM8K−GSM1K gap) perform WORSE\\\\n      on DIF-C items — DIF captures capability, not contamination.\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      Cross-benchmark validation of Claim 4's \\\\\\\"DIF as diagnostic lens\\\\\\\":\\\\n      contaminated models cannot fake performance on DIF items, which\\\\n      capture genuine capability-discriminative differences. The negative\\\\n      correlation inverts the contamination narrative.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Scatter: x = GSM8K−GSM1K contamination gap, y = DIF advantage\\\\n        (acc_DIF_C − acc_non_DIF). Regression line with 95% CI band.\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Color by model family (Phi green, Gemma orange, Llama blue,\\\\n        Mixtral purple, DeepSeek red, Other gray)\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Annotate: r=−0.478, p=0.005 (partial r=−0.385 ctrl accuracy)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Annotate key outlier: Deepseek-math-7b-rl (near zero on both)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Dashed horizontal line at DIF advantage = 0; most models below\\\\n        (DIF items are harder for all, but contaminated models worse)\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Already generated by Agent Worker — may need style refinement\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_004_gsm8k_cross_benchmark, artifacts/plan_004/gsm1k_dif_correlation.csv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n    source_file: \\\\\\\"artifacts/plan_004/fig_gsm1k_dif_correlation.pdf\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_A1_subject_heatmap\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >-\\\\n      Per-subject ETS C percentage for all 57 MMLU subjects (temporal\\\\n      cohort), showing pervasiveness of item instability\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      Item instability is pervasive across all MMLU subjects, not\\\\n      concentrated in a few — temporal DIF is a benchmark-wide phenomenon.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Horizontal bar chart sorted by C%, all 57 subjects\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Color-coded by domain group (STEM / humanities / social science\\\\n        / other) to show whether instability clusters by domain\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Vertical dashed line at overall mean C% (31.3%)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Annotate subjects with known capability jumps (e.g.,\\\\n        abstract_algebra, college_mathematics)\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_001_real_data_dif_mmlu\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: appendix\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"1:2\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_A2_q3_distribution\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: >-\\\\n      Q3 residual correlation distribution: within-subject vs\\\\n      cross-subject pairs\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      Local dependence exists (11.3% within-subject pairs exceed 0.20)\\\\n      but is mild — a contributing factor, not the main FPR driver.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - >-\\\\n        Overlapping density plot: within-subject Q3 (blue) vs\\\\n        cross-subject Q3 (orange)\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Vertical dashed line at |Q3|=0.20 threshold\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Annotate: 11.3% vs 2.2% above threshold (5x ratio)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Inset text: Mann-Whitney p=2.3e-9\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_013_q3_local_independence\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: appendix\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\ntable_plan:\\\\n  - table_id: tab_1_temporal_landscape\\\\n    caption: >-\\\\n      DIF detection results across cohort definitions on METABENCH MMLU\\\\n      (5,227 models × 12,508 items). ETS C = large DIF (|Δ_MH| ≥ 1.5).\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns:\\\\n      - Cohort Definition\\\\n      - N_ref / N_focal\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"ETS C (%)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"FPR (Li clean)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - Enrichment\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_001_real_data_dif_mmlu, plan_014_within_subject_dif\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      Rows: temporal (2023 vs 2024), ability-quintile (Q1-2 vs Q4-5),\\\\n      within-family (Llama-2 vs Llama-3), random split (sanity check),\\\\n      within-subject average (5 subjects). Highlight contrast: sanity\\\\n      0.18% vs temporal 31.3%. Within-subject 38.1% proves instability\\\\n      penetrates within domains.\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_2_structural_comparison\\\\n    caption: >-\\\\n      Three structural improvement attempts and their outcomes. None\\\\n      reduces temporal baseline FPR below 31%.\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns:\\\\n      - Approach\\\\n      - Mechanism\\\\n      - Temporal FPR\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"TPR (p=0.5)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - Outcome\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_001b_matched_ability_dif, plan_012_irt_theta_conditioning, plan_014_within_subject_dif\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      Rows: baseline (plan_011 external matching), matched-ability\\\\n      quintiles (plan_001b), IRT θ-conditioning (plan_012), within-subject\\\\n      pre-check (plan_014). Frame as \\\\\\\"empirical observation revealing\\\\n      structural constraint\\\\\\\" not \\\\\\\"our method failed.\\\\\\\" Note: higher-level\\\\n      methods (MIMIC, propensity-score) may address individual constraints.\\\\n    baseline_row: \\\\\\\"Baseline (plan_011 external matching, FPR=0.314, TPR=0.834)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_3_gsm8k_comparison\\\\n    caption: >-\\\\n      Cross-benchmark comparison: MMLU vs GSM8K on temporal DIF and\\\\n      semi-synthetic detection. GSM8K replicates key findings on a\\\\n      single-domain math benchmark (1,319 items × 6,702 models).\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns:\\\\n      - Metric\\\\n      - MMLU (plan_001/011)\\\\n      - GSM8K (plan_004)\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_001_real_data_dif_mmlu, plan_011_semi_synthetic_validation, plan_004_gsm8k_cross_benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      Rows: Items / Models / Temporal C% / Sanity C% / Semi-synthetic\\\\n      ΔTPR (standard, p=0.7) / ΔTPR (external, p=0.5) / Baseline FPR\\\\n      (external) / GSM1K gap correlation (GSM8K only). Highlight: GSM8K\\\\n      standard ΔTPR=+0.818 vs MMLU external ΔTPR=+0.508. Different\\\\n      matching strategies make direct comparison nuanced — note in caption.\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_A1_power_analysis\\\\n    caption: >-\\\\n      DIF power analysis: ETS C classification performance with real\\\\n      MMLU IRT parameters across sample sizes and effect sizes.\\\\n    placement: appendix\\\\n    type: statistics\\\\n    columns:\\\\n      - N per group\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"δ (logits)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - TPR (ETS C)\\\\n      - FPR (ETS C)\\\\n      - AUC (MH)\\\\n      - AUC (LR)\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: mvp_dif_power_sim, mvp_dif_power_sim_phase2\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      Grid: N ∈ {50, 80, 100} × δ ∈ {1.0, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6}. Operational\\\\n      region: N≥80, δ≥1.4 (TPR 0.68-0.80, FPR 0.036-0.042).\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_A2_within_subject_detail\\\\n    caption: >-\\\\n      Per-subject semi-synthetic validation with cross-subject vs\\\\n      within-subject matching at p=0.5.\\\\n    placement: appendix\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns:\\\\n      - Subject\\\\n      - N items\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Baseline FPR (cross)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"ΔTPR (cross)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Baseline FPR (within)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"ΔTPR (within)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"ΔFPR (cross)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      5 subjects: business_ethics, human_aging, professional_medicine,\\\\n      formal_logic, high_school_statistics. Cross-subject ΔFPR=0.000 for\\\\n      all subjects. business_ethics is failure case (baseline FPR>0.53\\\\n      both methods).\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_A3_threshold_sensitivity\\\\n    caption: >-\\\\n      ETS threshold sensitivity analysis with BH FDR correction (q=0.05).\\\\n      Finding robust across thresholds; BH correction removes only 3\\\\n      items at standard threshold.\\\\n    placement: appendix\\\\n    type: statistics\\\\n    columns:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"ETS Threshold (|Δ_MH|)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"C% (uncorrected)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"C% (BH-corrected)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"N items flagged\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_018_ets_threshold_sensitivity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      Rows: 1.0 (48.9%/48.2%), 1.5 (31.3%/31.3%), 2.0 (18.3%/18.3%),\\\\n      2.5 (10.2%/10.2%). BH has minimal effect because ETS dual\\\\n      threshold (effect size + significance) already controls FPR.\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_A4_circularity_ablation\\\\n    caption: >-\\\\n      Circularity ablation: ranking disruption from removing different\\\\n      item subsets (31.3% removal rate). DIF is least disruptive\\\\n      non-random strategy.\\\\n    placement: appendix\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns:\\\\n      - Removal Strategy\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"ρ (2024 cohort)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"z vs random\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Jaccard with DIF-C\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_020_circularity_ablation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      DIF z=-9.5 vs random, but high-discrimination z=-15.8,\\\\n      accuracy-gap z=-156.5, high-variance z=-107.9. DIF-discrimination\\\\n      Jaccard=0.12. DIF isolates ability-conditioned differential\\\\n      behavior, not just generally informative items.\\\\n\\\\nliterature_strategy:\\\\n  citation_budget: 40\\\\n  macro_context:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"LLM benchmark contamination detection and validation methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"psychometric methods (IRT, DIF) applied to LLM evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"benchmark temporal comparability and dynamic evaluation design\\\\\\\"\\\\n  micro_clusters:\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"Contamination Detection Methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"benchmark contamination detection LLM 2024 2025 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Min-K++ training data detection language model\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"ConStat perturbation contamination benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"n-gram overlap contamination benchmark LLM\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Wang et al. 2026 RL fragility (ICLR 2026)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Liang et al. 2026 DVD generation variance\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Tao et al. 2026 Self-Critique (ICLR 2026)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Li et al. EMNLP 2024 web-overlap labels\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Shi et al. 2024 Min-K++\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Balloccu et al. 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 10\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"IRT in LLM Evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"item response theory LLM evaluation 2024 2025 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Growing Pains fixed parameter calibration benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"psychometric analysis language model benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Habba et al. 2026 Growing Pains (NOT Polo)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Zhou et al. 2026 PSN-IRT AAAI (NOT Polo)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Hardy et al. 2026 scalability coefficients\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Lalor et al. 2019 IRT for NLP\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Vania et al. 2021\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 8\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"DIF Methodology & Measurement Invariance\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"differential item functioning Mantel-Haenszel ETS classification\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"impact versus DIF Shealy Stout SIBTEST\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"DIF small sample logistic regression\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Zumbo 1999 DIF\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"measurement invariance Meredith 1993\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Vandenberg Lance 2000 measurement invariance review\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Swaminathan Rogers 1990 non-uniform DIF\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"propensity score matching DIF Teresi 2007\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Lord chi-square DIF Raju area measure\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Holland & Thayer 1988 MH-DIF\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Shealy & Stout 1993 SIBTEST\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Clauser & Mazor 1998 DIF review\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Zumbo 1999 DIF overview\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Belzak 2020 small-sample DIF\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Kim & Kolen 2019 fixed parameter calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Meredith 1993 measurement invariance\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Vandenberg & Lance 2000 invariance review\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Swaminathan & Rogers 1990 non-uniform DIF\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Teresi 2007 propensity score DIF\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 12\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"Benchmark Design & Concurrent Work\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"contamination-free benchmark LLM LiveBench 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"benchmark ground truth contamination labels validation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"GSM1K GSM8K contamination evidence\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"GAIA benchmark LLM evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Chatbot Arena LLM ranking\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"MMLU benchmark analysis psychometric\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"White et al. 2025 LiveBench (ICLR)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Zhang et al. 2024 GSM1K\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"METABENCH original paper\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 8\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"LLM Evaluation Methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"LLM evaluation methodology benchmark design 2025 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"MMLU benchmark psychometric analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"language model evaluation survey\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite: []\\\\n      target_count: 5\\\\n\\\\nwriting_order:\\\\n  - experiments\\\\n  - method\\\\n  - introduction\\\\n  - related_work\\\\n  - discussion\\\\n  - abstract\\\\n  - conclusion\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 39998, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A5qKsPcGeo4aB38AEfAfLD","session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c","uuid":"63815f10-9921-4568-bd95-2c