{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DhXarwWWaWpH9d5HUNHz6y","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tThe null mean (0.22\\%) closely matches the random-split control (0.18\\%), confirming both as valid null estimates.\n201\t\n202\tThreshold sensitivity analysis shows C\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ thresholds \\{1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5\\} $=$ \\{48.9\\%, 31.3\\%, 18.3\\%, 10.2\\%\\}.\n203\tBenjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q=0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C items (31.32\\%$\\to$31.30\\%), confirming that the ETS dual requirement (effect size $+$ significance) provides near-complete implicit multiple-testing protection.\n204\t\n205\t\\subsection{Systematic Confound Elimination}\n206\t\\label{sec:confounds}\n207\t\n208\tWe systematically evaluate six potential confounds.\n209\t\n210\t\\paragraph{Architecture heterogeneity.}\n211\tMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227).\n212\tRestricting to decoder-only models yields identical results (C\\%$=$31.3\\%, Jaccard$=$1.0, 0 items changed).\n213\t\n214\t\\paragraph{Ability confound.}\n215\tThe temporal cohort ability gap is small (Cohen's $d=-0.17$, overlap$=$76.4\\%).\n216\tNearest-neighbor 1:1 matching ($d \\approx -0.01$) reduces C\\% from 31.3\\% to 28.9\\%$\\pm$0.1\\%---ability confound accounts for only 2.4\\,pp (7.7\\%).\n217\tThe within-family Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 contrast ($d=-0.99$) drops from 61.3\\% to 28.3\\% under matching.\n218\tBoth analyses converge to $\\sim$28--29\\% genuine item-level DIF.\n219\t\n220\t\\paragraph{Local independence.}\n221\tYen's Q3 statistics show negligible local dependence (mean Q3$=$0.009 within-subject, 2.2\\% of cross-subject pairs above 0.20).\n222\tRemoving 257 high-Q3 items changes C\\% by $<$0.5\\,pp.\n223\t\n224\t\\paragraph{Model non-independence.}\n225\tGraduated de-duplication (capping models per family at $K=\\{1,2,5,10,20\\}$) shows C\\% $\\leq$ random-subsample C\\% at every cap: $K=20$ yields 29.1\\% vs.\\ random 30.9\\%; $K=5$ yields 11.7\\% vs.\\ random 16.9\\%.\n226\tFamily structure does not inflate C\\%.\n227\t\n228\t\\paragraph{IRT $\\theta$-conditioning.}\n229\tReplacing CTT total-score matching with IRT $\\theta$-conditioning ($r=0.9986$ between $\\theta$ and total score) yields identical results: temporal FPR$=$0.312 vs.\\ 0.314 with CTT.\n230\t\n231\t\\paragraph{Non-uniform DIF.}\n232\tLogistic-regression DIF on 9 subjects (1,787 items) shows 92.1\\% concordance with MH uniform classifications.\n233\tNon-uniform DIF adds 24.8\\% (effect-size controlled), making 31\\% a conservative lower bound.\n234\t\n235\t\\begin{table}[t]\n236\t\\centering\n237\t\\caption{Logistic regression models predicting DIF-C classification. OR per 0.1-unit change. Simpson's paradox: domain $\\Delta$acc reverses from OR$=$1.55 to OR$=$0.73 when item properties are included.}\n238\t\\label{tab:regression}\n239\t\\small\n240\t\\begin{tabular}{llrl}\n241\t\\toprule\n242\tModel & Predictors & $R^2_{\\text{McF}}$ & Key OR [95\\% CI] \\\\\n243\t\\midrule\n244\tM1 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & 0.0007 & 1.55 [1.19, 2.03] \\\\\n245\tM2 & M1 + diff.\\ + disc.\\ & 0.0423 & 0.73 [0.55, 0.97] \\\\\n246\tM3 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 0.0012 & 1.08 \\\\\n247\tM4 & M3 + diff.\\ + disc.\\ & 0.0434 & 0.92 [0.69, 1.22] \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\end{table}\n251\t\n252\tTable~\\ref{tab:regression} presents the item-level decomposition.\n253\tDomain accuracy improvement shows a marginal association with DIF (M1: OR$=$1.55, $R^2=0.0007$), but this reverses when item difficulty and discrimination are included (M2: OR$=$0.73).\n254\tThis Simpson's paradox confirms that domain-level improvement is not the driver of item-level DIF.\n255\tItem discrimination is the true predictor (OR$=$1.51--1.54), which is psychometrically expected: more discriminating items are more sensitive to group differences.\n256\tEven the full model (M4) explains only 7.4\\% of DIF variance---92.6\\% remains item-specific.\n257\t\n258\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication}\n259\t\\label{sec:crossbench}\n260\t\n261\t\\begin{table}[t]\n262\t\\centering\n263\t\\caption{Temporal DIF across six METABENCH benchmarks. All show substantial DIF far exceeding random controls ($<$1\\%).}\n264\t\\label{tab:sixbench}\n265\t\\small\n266\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrc}\n267\t\\toprule\n268\tBenchmark & $N_{\\text{items}}$ & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & C\\% & Enrich.\\ \\\\\n269\t\\midrule\n270\tMMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 174$\\times$ \\\\\n271\tWinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 30.3 & 95$\\times$ \\\\\n272\tTruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 29.7 & 80$\\times$ \\\\\n273\tARC-Challenge & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 26.9 & 45$\\times$ \\\\\n274\tGSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 & 63$\\times$ \\\\\n275\tHellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.9 & 64$\\times$ \\\\\n276\t\\bottomrule\n277\t\\end{tabular}\n278\t\\end{table}\n279\t\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:sixbench} shows temporal DIF across six benchmarks.\n281\tAll exhibit substantial instability (17.9--31.3\\%), with random controls uniformly below 1\\% and enrichment ratios $\\geq$45$\\times$.\n282\tKnowledge-intensive benchmarks (MMLU, TruthfulQA) show higher rates than reasoning benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag), an interpretable pattern consistent with training data vintage effects.\n283\t\n284\t\\begin{table}[t]\n285\t\\centering\n286\t\\caption{GSM8K cross-benchmark analysis. Semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR confirms framework portability. GSM1K correlation is exploratory ($N=33$).}\n287\t\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n288\t\\small\n289\t\\begin{tabular}{llr}\n290\t\\toprule\n291\tAnalysis & Metric & Value \\\\\n292\t\\midrule\n293\tTemporal DIF & C\\% & 18.9 \\\\\n294\tTemporal DIF & Random control C\\% & 0.3 \\\\\n295\tSemi-synthetic & $\\Delta$TPR ($p=1.0$) & 0.818 \\\\\n296\tSemi-synthetic & $\\Delta$FPR & 0.0 \\\\\n297\tGSM1K correlation & Pearson $r$ & $-0.478$ ($p=0.005$) \\\\\n298\tGSM1K correlation & $N$ models & 33 \\\\\n299\t\\bottomrule\n300\t\\end{tabular}\n301\t\\end{table}\n302\t\n303\tTable~\\ref{tab:gsm8k} details the GSM8K analysis.\n304\tThe semi-synthetic framework achieves $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818 (higher than MMLU's +0.674 due to cleaner single-domain matching).\n305\tGSM1K contamination-gap correlation ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$) is negative---opposite to a simple contamination hypothesis---suggesting DIF captures genuine functioning changes rather than memorization.\n306\tWe note this as exploratory given the small sample ($N=33$, CI: [$-0.71$, $-0.16$]) and leave confirmation to future work.\n307\t\n308\tHalf-year temporal windows (2023-H2 vs.\\ 2024-H1) yield C\\%$=$34.35\\% on MMLU---higher than the full-year rate---indicating DIF saturates within 6 months.\n309\tThis pattern holds across all benchmarks, informing a practical monitoring frequency for audit protocols.\n310\t\n311\t\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\n312\t\\label{sec:metacritique}\n313\t\n314\tDirect comparison of DIF-C flags against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items reveals statistical independence: enrichment$=$0.964, Jaccard$=$0.206, $\\phi=0.012$, $\\chi^2=1.28$ ($p=0.258$), OR$=$1.05 [0.96, 1.15].\n315\tDIF flags and web-overlap labels measure fundamentally different constructs: web-overlap captures \\emph{whether} an item is publicly accessible, while DIF captures \\emph{whether} an item functions differently across cohorts.\n316\tEnrichment$\\approx$1.0 is the expected outcome under our meta-critique, not evidence of DIF failure.\n317\t\n318\tThis has field-wide implications: all item-level contamination detectors validated exclusively against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, ConStat, and n-gram methods---have this validation blind spot.\n319\tSemi-synthetic injection confirms DIF \\emph{can} detect differential contamination ($\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 at $p=0.5$ with cross-subject matching), demonstrating that the method works for its intended purpose even though web-overlap labels cannot verify this.\n320\t\n321\t\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n322\t\\label{sec:validation}\n323\t\n324\t\\begin{table}[t]\n325\t\\centering\n326\t\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: $\\Delta$TPR and $\\Delta$FPR by matching strategy. Cross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 (Proposition~1).}\n327\t\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n328\t\\small\n329\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n330\t\\toprule\n331\tMatching & $\\Delta$FPR & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.03}$ & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.05}$ & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.10}$ \\\\\n332\t\\midrule\n333\tCross-subject & 0.000 & 0.222 & 0.508 & 0.674 \\\\\n334\tWithin-subject & 0.186 & 0.134 & 0.298 & 0.302 \\\\\n335\tCross-benchmark & 0.000 & -- & -- & 0.818 \\\\\n336\t\\bottomrule\n337\t\\end{tabular}\n338\t\\end{table}\n339\t\n340\tTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} presents the semi-synthetic validation results.\n341\tCross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all 5 tested subjects, empirically confirming Proposition~1.\n342\tWithin-subject matching suffers from substantial FPR inflation (+0.186) due to matching variable contamination, demonstrating that the choice of matching strategy is not merely technical but has fundamental validity implications.\n343\t\n344\tThe dose-response curve is monotonic with inflection at $p \\approx 0.35$ and saturation at $p \\approx 0.7$, providing an operational characterization of DIF sensitivity: moderate contamination ($p=0.5$) is reliably detected, while low-level contamination ($p<0.3$) falls below the detection threshold.\n345\t\n346\tCross-benchmark portability is confirmed by GSM8K replication ($\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818, $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000), establishing the framework as a reusable validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector on multi-domain benchmarks.\n347\t\n348\t\\subsection{Downstream Impact and Diagnostic Lens}\n349\t\\label{sec:downstream}\n350\t\n351\t\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.}\n352\tRemoving all 3,918 DIF-C items yields Spearman $\\rho=0.9965$ with full-benchmark rankings---MMLU has massive measurement redundancy.\n353\tProgressive removal analysis confirms graceful degradation: $\\rho=0.9998$ at 5\\%, 0.9976 at 20\\%, and 0.9891 even at 50\\% removal.\n354\tCronbach's $\\alpha$ drops from 0.994 to 0.990 at 31\\% removal; construct coverage remains at 96\\%.\n355\t\n356\t\\paragraph{DIF items carry cross-cohort signal.}\n357\tDIF removal is more disruptive than random removal across all 21 conditions (7 removal fractions $\\times$ 3 metrics; mean $z=-14.91$).\n358\tHowever, circularity ablation shows DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal is 16$\\times$ more disruptive ($z=-157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance 11$\\times$ ($z=-108$), and high-discrimination 1.7$\\times$ ($z=-16$).\n359\tDespite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH ability conditioning isolates genuine differential functioning rather than raw group differences (DIF-discrimination Jaccard$=$0.12).\n360\t\n361\t\\paragraph{Item characterization.}\n362\tDIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's $d=-0.247$) and higher-discrimination ($d=0.230$) but uniformly distributed across domains ($\\chi^2$ $p=0.32$).\n363\tThe key finding is a difficulty$\\times$direction interaction ($\\rho=-0.597$): 88.7\\% of hardest-decile DIF items favor 2024 models, while 96.2\\% of easiest-decile items favor 2023 models.\n364\tThis reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes.\n365\t\n366\t\\paragraph{DIF-cleaned benchmark demonstration.}\n367\tRemoving 3,918 DIF-C items from MMLU produces systematic cohort asymmetry ($z=8.5$ vs.\\ random baseline $z<1.0$): 2024 models lose an average of 73.6 ranks, while 2023 models gain 22.6.\n368\tIndividual model impacts are substantial (phi-3: $-$258 ranks; mistral derivatives: $+$60).\n369\tThis demonstrates that instability, while not threatening overall ranking validity, introduces measurable cohort-specific bias---precisely the diagnostic signal that benchmark maintainers need.\n370\t\n371\t% ============================================================\n372\t% 5. LIMITATIONS\n373\t% ============================================================\n374\t\\section{Limitations}\n375\t\n376\tOur analysis has three main limitations.\n377\tFirst, 1D IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (RMSEA$>$0.08).\n378\tOur attempt at bifactor modeling failed due to inadequate fit, motivating future multidimensional IRT work.\n379\tSecond, the semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination processes; DIF detection power for subtle contamination patterns remains uncharacterized.\n380\tThird, reasoning-RL robustness is supported only by directional evidence ($N=13$ RL models added to $N=820$ cohort, $\\Delta$C\\%$=$0.1\\,pp) and theoretical arguments, not by a powered empirical test.\n381\t\n382\t% ============================================================\n383\t% 6. CONCLUSION\n384\t% ============================================================\n385\t\\section{Conclusion}\n386\t\n387\tWe present the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks, revealing that 31\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable across model generations.\n388\tThis instability is genuine---not an artifact of capability improvement, architecture heterogeneity, model non-independence, or statistical procedure---and replicates across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\n389\tThe field's web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; our semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0) provides the needed validation infrastructure.\n390\tDIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: benchmark maintainers can identify which items and capability dimensions are shifting, enabling principled benchmark maintenance without sacrificing measurement validity.\n391\t\n392\t% ============================================================\n393\t% REFERENCES (placeholder)\n394\t% ============================================================\n395\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n396\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n397\t\n398\t\\bibitem[Hendrycks et~al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021mmlu}\n399\tD.~Hendrycks, C.~Burns, S.~Basart, A.~Zou, M.~Mazeika, D.~Song, and J.~Steinhardt.\n400\t\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n401\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n402\t\n403\t\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988alternate}\n404\tP.~W. Holland and D.~T. Thayer.\n405\t\\newblock Differential item performance and the {M}antel--{H}aenszel procedure.\n406\t\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n407\t\n408\t\\bibitem[Holland and Wainer(1993)]{holland1993differential}\n409\tP.~W. Holland and H.~Wainer.\n410\t\\newblock \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}.\n411\t\\newblock Lawrence Erlbaum, 1993.\n412\t\n413\t\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\n414\tY.~Li, Y.~Dong, R.~Xiao, D.~Yin, and Y.~Zhang.\n415\t\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n416\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2024.\n417\t\n418\t\\bibitem[Mantel and Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\n419\tN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n420\t\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n421\t\\newblock \\emph{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n422\t\n423\t\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\n424\tY.~Perlitz, E.~Bandel, and L.~Ein-Dor.\n425\t\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale benchmark of model evaluations.\n426\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.10108}, 2024.\n427\t\n428\t\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024growing}\n429\tF.~M. Polo, R.~Fonseca, L.~Weber, and others.\n430\t\\newblock Growing pains: On the scalability of adaptive evaluation.\n431\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n432\t\n433\t\\bibitem[Zieky(1993)]{zieky1993practical}\n434\tM.~Zieky.\n435\t\\newblock Practical questions in the use of {DIF} statistics in test development.\n436\t\\newblock In \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--347. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1993.\n437\t\n438\t\\end{thebibliography}\n439\t\n440\t% ============================================================\n441\t% APPENDIX\n442\t% ============================================================\n443\t\\appendix\n444\t\\section{Supplementary Experiments}\n445\t\\label{app:supplementary}\n446\t\n447\t\\paragraph{RL robustness (\\S\\ref{sec:confounds}).}\n448\tAdding 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) to the focal cohort changes C\\% by only 0.1\\,pp (31.2\\% vs.\\ 31.1\\% baseline; Jaccard$=$0.94).\n449\tTraditional detectors degrade substantially on RL models (Min-K\\% AUROC: $0.72 \\to 0.51$, DeLong $p=0.03$; ConStat: $0.68 \\to 0.43$, $p=0.008$), while DIF remains stable (AUROC: $0.92 \\to 0.91$, $p=0.88$).\n450\tPower analysis shows the partial-exploitation detection boundary at $p \\approx 0.45$.\n451\t\n452\t\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\n453\tIFT analysis with continuous release-date covariates yields 33.8\\% detection rate (Jaccard$=$0.78, $\\kappa=0.74$ with MH).\n454\tDIF onset concentrates in 2023-Q4 ($\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$ increases 0.08/month with inflection at 2023-Q4), suggesting training-data-cutoff-related temporal boundaries.\n455\t\n456\t\\paragraph{Graceful degradation.}\n457\tProgressive item removal shows $\\rho \\geq 0.997$ at 31\\% and $\\geq 0.989$ at 50\\%.\n458\tDIF-targeted removal is slightly more disruptive than random ($z=-9.5$) but well within the theoretical bound of $1-(r/N)^2$.\n459\t\n460\t\\paragraph{Cumulative Bayesian DIF (simulation).}\n461\tAcross 4 simulated administrations ($N=200$ per cohort per wave), TPR for medium effects ($\\delta=1.0$) grows from 0.40 (wave~1) to 0.78 (wave~4), while FPR remains stable ($\\leq$0.045).\n462\tBayes factors accumulate from median$=$3.2 (wave~1) to 28.5 (wave~3), demonstrating the practical viability of longitudinal DIF monitoring.\n463\t\n464\t\\paragraph{Killed/blocked experiments.}\n465\tBifactor IRT equating (RMSEA$>$0.08, CFI$<$0.90) failed due to MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality.\n466\tItem generation calibration was blocked by bifactor failure.\n467\tEnsemble fusion (DIF$+$ConStat$+$DVD) was dropped with the pivot from detection to characterization.\n468\tThese negative results motivated the diagnostic-lens framing adopted in this paper.\n469\t\n470\t\\end{document}\n471\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A5qKsPcGeo4aB38AEfAfLD","session_id":"bb24cef7-36bb-4a79-8344-c199f758081c","uuid":"1713ef6a-c523-4c31-b541-c06ad49ee189","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:14:59.130Z"}
